\script {SH Lazy Workflow Master}

\keywords {
   Space Hunter, OSC, workflow, Lazy Workflow, MGC, GradientCorrection, background model,
   SPFC, SPCC, BlurXTerminator
}

\author {
   Georg G. Albrecht
}

\copyright {
   2026 Georg G. Albrecht. MIT License. AutoColor.js \copy 2016 Hartmut V. Bornemann, used with written permission.
}

\brief {
   Runs SH Lazy Workflow Part 1 and Part 2 as one workflow: Part 2 starts by itself as soon as you apply a background model or GC result in Part 1.
}

\description {

   The Master runs the two parts of the Space Hunter Lazy Workflow,
   \sref {SH Lazy Workflow Part 1} {Part 1} and
   \sref {SH Lazy Workflow Part 2} {Part 2}, as one workflow. You set up both
   parts at the start; after that the run only needs you once, to choose
   the background model (or GC result) in Part 1's panel. As soon as you
   click \s Apply there, Part 2 continues without being started by hand.

   The Master contains both parts unchanged. Every step works and is set up
   exactly as described in their manuals. This manual only covers what the
   Master adds: the start dialog, and when a run stops and how it continues.

   \s {Master sequence:} start dialog &rarr; Part 1 dialog &rarr; Part 2
   dialog &rarr; Part 1 (plate solve, SPFC, previews, MGC / GC) &rarr; your
   choice in the model / GC panel &rarr; Part 2 (BXT correct, background,
   SPCC, stretch, BXT full, and the optional steps).
}

\section { Requirements, Files and License } {

   \subsection { \label requirements Requirements } {

      The same as for Part 1 and Part 2 together: see \e {Requirements} in
      the \sref {SH Lazy Workflow Part 1} {Part 1} and
      \sref {SH Lazy Workflow Part 2} {Part 2} manuals. The Master has no
      requirements of its own.
   }

   \subsection { \label access Files, Programs and Internet Access } {

      Everything Part 1 and Part 2 store, write or download is listed in
      \e {Files, Programs and Internet Access} in their manuals, and applies
      unchanged when they run inside the Master. The Master adds only this:

      \list[spaced] {
         { \s {Settings:} the Master stores no settings of its own.
           Part 1's and Part 2's
           dialogs use the same saved settings as the single scripts, so what
           you choose in one is remembered in the other. }
         { \s {Image:} the Master itself writes nothing to the image. It reads
           the re-run marks and the processing history to fill in
           \e {Already done}. }
         { \s {Programs:} none of its own. }
         { \s {Internet:} none of its own. }
      }
   }

   \subsection { \label license License } {

      The Master is released under the MIT License, Copyright \copy 2026
      Georg G. Albrecht, like Part 1 and Part 2. It contains Part 2, and with
      it the AutoColor code (\copy 2016 Hartmut V. Bornemann, used with
      written permission), which must be kept in its original form and needs
      a separate written agreement with its author. See the
      \xref {https://github.com/ggalb/SpaceHunter_Scripts/blob/main/LICENSE} {LICENSE}
      on GitHub.
   }
}

\usage {

   \subsection { \label start The Start Dialog } {

      \center {
         \image[width:480px] images/Master_0.png
      }

      The start dialog shows the active image and what has already been done
      to it, and lets you choose what to run.

      \definition {
         { Already done } { Read from the image before anything runs:
                            plate solution, Ha/OIII separation, SPFC, gradient
                            correction and Part 2's steps. A step counts only
                            while it is still active in the History Explorer
                            (see \lref detect {What counts as done}). }
         { Part 1 + Part 2 } { The whole workflow. Preselected when no
                            gradient correction was found. }
         { Part 1 only }    { Part 1 as the single script would run it. }
         { Part 2 only }    { Continue after the gradient correction.
                            Preselected when a gradient correction or a
                            Part 2 step was found. }
      }

      After \s OK, Part 1's dialog opens, then Part 2's (for
      \e {Part 1 + Part 2}). Both are the dialogs of the single scripts.
      Part 2's camera, filter set and white reference start with what you
      chose for SPFC in Part 1, if Part 2's list has the same entry; check
      them before you click OK. \s Cancel in any dialog ends the Master
      without changing the image.

      \note {
         \e {New Instance} in Part 1's or Part 2's dialog makes a process icon
         of the Master, not of the single script: double-click it (or apply
         it globally) to start the Master with those settings. An icon from
         Part 2's dialog holds the settings of both parts; one from Part 1's
         dialog holds only Part 1's, because Part 2's dialog has not been
         shown yet at that point.
      }
   }

   \subsection { \label run The Run } {

      With \e {Part 1 + Part 2}, everything is set up first, then the image is
      processed:

      \list[ordered, spaced] {
         { The start dialog, Part 1's dialog and Part 2's dialog. }
         { Part 2's background pre-check (IFN / dust map), if it is switched
           on and the image is already plate solved. }
         { Part 1 runs: plate solve, SPFC, previews, MGC (or GC). }
         { Part 1's \e {Choose background model} panel (or \e {Choose GC
           result}) opens. This is the one moment the run waits for you. }
         { After \s Apply, Part 2 starts by itself. On an image that was not
           plate solved at the start, its background pre-check comes now,
           because the dust map needs the image's coordinates. }
         { Part 2 runs to the end. }
      }

      If MGC fails (for example where the MARS database has no data), Part 1
      falls back to GradientCorrection as usual, and the Master waits for
      \s Apply in the GC result panel instead.
   }

   \subsection { \label stops When the Run Stops } {

      The Master stops before Part 2, with a message in the console, when:

      \list[spaced] {
         { \s {ImageSolver needs you:} the image could not be plate solved
           automatically. Solve it, then run the Master again. }
         { \s {DBE is ticked in Part 1:} DBE is applied by hand, so Part 2's
           dialog is not shown at all in that run. Place the samples, apply
           DBE, then run the Master again. }
         { \s {The panel is closed without Apply,} or the MGC / GC tool is
           opened instead: nothing was applied. Apply a gradient correction,
           then run the Master again. }
      }

      In each case the next run sees what was done and preselects
      \e {Part 2 only}, so it continues where it stopped. A DBE, MGC, GC or
      ABE step that is active in the History Explorer counts, as does
      Part 1's own mark.
   }

   \subsection { \label detect What Counts as Done } {

      \list[spaced] {
         { Part 1's and Part 2's re-run marks (see their manuals) count while
           their step is still active in the History Explorer. Going back in
           the History Explorer undoes them; the Master then offers those
           steps again. }
         { A DBE, MGC, GC or ABE tool step counts while it is active in the
           History Explorer. }
         { \s {Reopened files:} a tool step from an earlier session leaves no
           trace once the file is closed; only the marks are saved (in XISF
           files). Choose \e {Part 2 only} yourself then. }
         { \s {GraXpert:} its background extraction leaves only a PixelMath
           step that copies another image in. The Master shows a hint
           (\e {PixelMath copy from another image in history}) but doesn't
           count it, because GraXpert's denoising leaves the same step. If it
           was a background extraction, choose \e {Part 2 only}. }
      }

      If you choose \e {Part 1 + Part 2} on an image that already has a
      gradient correction, Part 1 and the Master warn you first. \s No is the
      default: a second gradient correction may damage the image.
   }
}

\relatedscripts {
   SH Lazy Workflow Part 1, SH Lazy Workflow Part 2, SH +Previews
}

\relatedtools {
   MultiscaleGradientCorrection, GradientCorrection, DynamicBackgroundExtraction
}

\make
